<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aelia Capitolina</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 130–4th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aelia Capitolina was the Roman colony founded by the emperor Hadrian on the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> around 130 CE, with a temple to Jupiter Capitolinus. The plan is often seen as a cause of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Bar Kokhba</a> revolt of 132–135, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were barred from the city. The name remained in official use for centuries, surviving in early Islamic times as Iliya.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Hadrian visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> in 129 to 130 CE. Jerusalem, once heavily rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a>, was still in ruins from the siege of 70 CE in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-jewish-roman-war">First Jewish–Roman War</a>, and the emperor resolved to refound it as a Roman colony dedicated to Jupiter. The name Aelia came from Hadrian's own Aelia gens, and Capitolina meant that the city was dedicated to Jupiter Capitolinus, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> believed had vanquished and replaced the God of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. Mary E. Smallwood thinks the foundation was likely an attempt to combat resurgent Jewish nationalism, and Martin Goodman describes it as a deliberate effort to end Jewish rebelliousness by erasing the city.</p><p>Ancient sources disagree on whether the foundation caused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Bar Kokhba</a> revolt of 132 to 135 or followed it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a> says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> objected to gentiles being settled in their city and foreign cults established there. Eusebius of Caesarea presents the colony as a punishment after the defeat. Coins of Aelia Capitolina struck before the revolt have convinced most modern historians that the foundation came first.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city was rebuilt on a Roman plan with a cardo and a decumanus, adapted to the western hill and the Temple Mount, and its two cardines met near the Damascus Gate, where a columnar monument gave the gate its Arabic name, Bab el-Amud. Hadrian placed the main forum at the junction of the main roads and built a large temple to Venus beside it, on a site later used for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a>. The colony had no walls, and a detachment of Legio X Fretensis guarded it.</p><p>Its population, about 4,000 by one estimate, was mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionaries</a>, veterans and other non-Jewish settlers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were barred on pain of death except on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisha-bav">Tisha B'Av</a>, and a pilgrim from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> in 333 saw them gather yearly to anoint a perforated stone and mourn. Under Constantine, in the early 4th century, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a> began the city's change into a Christian centre, and it became the fifth patriarchate. The ban on Jews lasted until the Muslim conquest of 636. In Arabic the name survived as Ilya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Roman street pattern is preserved in the Old City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Substantial remains of the western cardo have been exposed near Suq el-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">Bazaar</a>, and walls of Hadrian's temple to Venus have been found beneath the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aelia Capitolina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The revolt broke out in 132, probably provoked by Hadrian's plan to refound Jerusalem as the Roman colony <b>Aelia Capitolina</b>, and possibly by a ban on circumcision.</blockquote><a href="/state/bar-kokhba-state">Bar Kokhba state</a></li></ul></section>
